From 7984cdeb242a9d6e2b1b0b9555191908809a51f6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t5dr <t5dr@local>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 09:59:58 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] 93-IOP-idle-fast-path-and-IOP-profile

Tekken 4 port (t4iop): the EE advances the emulated IOP at every checkpoint, ~50,000 times a fight
frame, and 99.6 % of those calls run no IOP instruction: the kernel's sealed idle window covers the
step, no SIF command is queued and no SPU2 sample (one per 768 IOP cycles) is due. runCycles still
went through its loop, the SIF queue check, drainMixedAudio, advanceTo, the SPU2 IRQ service and a
4 KB stack buffer each time (~0.75 ms of a ~14 ms fight frame on a 7950X). IopEmulator::runEeCycles
now does what runCycles does in that case -- move the clock and bump the idle-skip counter (it is
in the hashed IOP scalars) -- in a few compares; a queued SIF command, the window ending inside the
step, a sample due, an IRQ bit set or an undecided sound route take the full path. The IOP state is
the same bit for bit (unit test: 10 wake sources x mixer on/off x 6,000 uneven advances, same
hashState; game: TEKKEN4_RB_IOPSTATE=1 frame hashes identical over 7,038 frames).
TEKKEN4_IOP_FAST_IDLE=0 = the full path every time.

Also PS2X_IOP_PROF=1 (include/ps2x/iop/iop_profile.h): TSC split of every IOP advance and RPC
(interpreter slices, HLE imports by library, SPU2 mix + IRQ, scheduler/SIF/DMA/timers), a
[iop-prof] line per 600 guest frames, iopProfileNs() for the rollback frame log's iop column;
PS2X_IOP_PROF_PCS=1 adds a histogram of the interpreted PCs. Off: one static bool per call.
